{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"025f8a41d481f474","name":"card_unit_seagull","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/WorldAtWar/units/card_unit_seagull","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SEAGULL","text":"Your HQ cannot lose more than 4 defense each turn.","flavorText":"The Curtiss SO3C Seamew was an American aircraft that was developed for the United States Navy as its standard floatplane scout. The manufacturer called the aircraft the Seamew, but in 1941 the US Navy started calling it the Seagull, which was the name of the Curtiss SOC biplane it was supposed to replace. Those in service of the Royal Navy were still called Seamew and this caused some confusion. From the time it entered service the SO3C suffered serious flaws, both with in-flight stability and with the Ranger engine. The in-flight stability problem was solved to some extent, but the engine problem remained, and its poor performance led to the aircraft being withdrawn from front line service. In some cases the SO3C was replaced with the old SOC Seagull, the one it was supposed to replace. In 1945 the Curtiss SO3C Seamew was retired and by that time a total of 795 aircraft had been produced, including variants.","faction":"USA","type":"fighter","rarity":"Rare","cost":2,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":4},"defaults":{"Name":"card_unit_seagull","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_unit_seagull_title","SourceString":"SEAGULL","LocalizedString":"SEAGULL"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":1,"defense":4,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_unit_seagull_text","SourceString":"Your HQ cannot lose more than 4 defense each turn.","LocalizedString":"Your HQ cannot lose more than 4 defense each turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_unit_seagull_history","SourceString":"The Curtiss SO3C Seamew was an American aircraft that was developed for the United States Navy as its standard floatplane scout. The manufacturer called the aircraft the Seamew, but in 1941 the US Navy started calling it the Seagull, which was the name of the Curtiss SOC biplane it was supposed to replace. Those in service of the Royal Navy were still called Seamew and this caused some confusion. From the time it entered service the SO3C suffered serious flaws, both with in-flight stability and with the Ranger engine. The in-flight stability problem was solved to some extent, but the engine problem remained, and its poor performance led to the aircraft being withdrawn from front line service. In some cases the SO3C was replaced with the old SOC Seagull, the one it was supposed to replace. In 1945 the Curtiss SO3C Seamew was retired and by that time a total of 795 aircraft had been produced, including variants.","LocalizedString":"The Curtiss SO3C Seamew was an American aircraft that was developed for the United States Navy as its standard floatplane scout. The manufacturer called the aircraft the Seamew, but in 1941 the US Navy started calling it the Seagull, which was the name of the Curtiss SOC biplane it was supposed to replace. Those in service of the Royal Navy were still called Seamew and this caused some confusion. From the time it entered service the SO3C suffered serious flaws, both with in-flight stability and with the Ranger engine. The in-flight stability problem was solved to some extent, but the engine problem remained, and its poor performance led to the aircraft being withdrawn from front line service. In some cases the SO3C was replaced with the old SOC Seagull, the one it was supposed to replace. In 1945 the Curtiss SO3C Seamew was retired and by that time a total of 795 aircraft had been produced, including variants."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::WorldAtWar","isReserved":true,"EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardReceiveDamage","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn","EReg